---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import GammaShielding from "../components/calc/GammaShielding.tsx";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { EX } from "../data/worked-examples";
import { u } from "../lib/site";
const G = EX.gamma;
---
<ToolPage slug="gamma-shielding">
  <GammaShielding slot="tool" client:only="react" />
  <Fragment slot="prose">
    <h2>How the dose rate is obtained</h2>
    <p>
      Most calculators of this kind look up a gamma constant in a table. This one computes it. For
      each photon line of the nuclide, the energy and the emission probability come from the
      evaluated nuclear data, and the mass energy-absorption coefficient of air at that energy comes
      from the NIST tables. Summing E·y·(µ<sub>en</sub>/ρ) over the lines and dividing by 4π gives
      the air kerma rate constant directly. The result is not set against published gamma constants,
      which describe a different quantity and often do not state the cutoff they apply. The
      {" "}<a href={u("/validation/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">validation report</a> checks the
      parts instead: the unit chain by hand, the interpolation of the NIST tables, and a sample of
      emission lines against an independent evaluation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Computing rather than copying has a practical benefit beyond provenance: the low-energy cutoff
      becomes a control you can move. Published constants apply a cutoff, usually 20 keV, to exclude
      photons that the source encapsulation absorbs before they reach anyone. That choice is
      invisible in a table and decisive in the answer. For Am-241 a 10 keV cutoff instead of 20 keV
      raises the constant by roughly a factor of eight, because the 17 keV neptunium L X-rays are
      absorbed strongly in air and dominate the kerma integral while contributing nothing at a metre
      through any real capsule.
    </p>

    <h2>Attenuation, and why it is not enough</h2>
    <p>
      A shield removes photons following exp(−µx), where µ is the linear attenuation coefficient —
      the mass attenuation coefficient at that energy multiplied by the density. The calculator
      applies this line by line, so a nuclide with a broad spectrum hardens correctly as it passes
      through: the low-energy lines are removed preferentially and the average energy behind the
      shield is higher than in front of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exponential attenuation alone describes a narrow beam, where any photon that interacts is gone.
      A real shield is a broad beam. Photons Compton-scatter inside the material, change direction,
      and a substantial fraction still emerge toward the receptor. Ignoring them can underestimate
      the dose rate behind a thick shield by a factor of several. The half-value layers this tool
      reports are therefore narrow-beam values, noticeably thinner than the broad-beam figures in
      shielding handbooks — 5.5 mm rather than about 6.5 mm of lead at 662 keV. The note on
      {" "}<a href={u("/half-value-layers/")} class="underline">half-value and tenth-value layers</a>
      {" "}tabulates these layers for six common emitters in lead, iron and concrete.
    </p>

    <h3>Buildup factors</h3>
    <p>
      The correction is the buildup factor B, the ratio of the true dose rate to the attenuated-only
      dose rate. It grows with the number of mean free paths and depends on material, energy and
      geometry. This calculator implements the two-parameter Berger form B = 1 + a·µx·e<sup>b·µx</sup>
      but ships no coefficients. The most widely used tabulation, the geometric-progression fit, is
      the body of a paid consensus standard, and reproducing it here would not be legitimate. Enter
      a and b from the reference you are working to, and the tool will show what difference they make.
    </p>
    <p>
      With attenuation only, the shield thickness this calculator returns is a lower bound. Treat it
      as the starting point for a design, never as the design.
    </p>

    <h2>Geometry assumptions</h2>
    <p>
      A point source in air, radiating isotropically, with a receptor at a stated distance and the
      shield between them. No room scatter, no skyshine, no source self-absorption, no capsule, and
      no correction from air kerma to a dose quantity in tissue. Real sources are extended, and close
      to an extended source the inverse-square falloff does not hold — near a large plane the dose
      rate barely falls with distance at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <h3>A caesium-137 source on an open bench</h3>
    <p>
      A 37&nbsp;GBq (1&nbsp;Ci) Cs-137 calibration source is lifted out of its storage pot for a
      check and stands on a bench. How high is the air kerma rate where people stand, and what would
      a 5&nbsp;cm lead block in front of it change?
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Input</th><th class="text-right">Air kerma rate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cs-137, 37&nbsp;GBq, 1&nbsp;m, no shield</td><td class="num text-right">{G.cs1m}&nbsp;mGy/h</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same source at 2&nbsp;m</td><td class="num text-right">{G.cs2m}&nbsp;mGy/h</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1&nbsp;m behind 5&nbsp;cm lead, attenuation only</td><td class="num text-right">{G.csLeadUGyH}&nbsp;µGy/h</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Doubling the distance divides the rate by four, as the inverse-square law says it must for a
      point source. The lead lets through {G.csLeadPct}% of the photons that travel straight through
      it, but that figure leaves out the photons scattered inside the block. Before the number is used
      to plan work, switch to “With buildup” and enter coefficients for lead at 662&nbsp;keV; the
      shielded rate rises, and the honest answer is the higher one. The block also has to be wide
      enough to cover the whole line of sight from the source to the people it protects; placed close
      to the source, a small block does that job.
    </p>

    <h3>Where to rope off an iridium-192 exposure</h3>
    <p>
      Site radiography uses a 1.5&nbsp;TBq Ir-192 source in a panoramic exposure with no collimator.
      Local rules put the barrier where the air kerma rate falls to 20&nbsp;µGy/h. Enter 1500&nbsp;GBq
      and 20&nbsp;m with no shield.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Quantity</th><th class="text-right">Result</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Air kerma rate at 20&nbsp;m</td><td class="num text-right">{G.ir20mUGyH}&nbsp;µGy/h</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Distance for 20&nbsp;µGy/h</td><td class="num text-right">{G.irBoundaryM}&nbsp;m</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The second line is one inverse-square step from the first: d = 20&nbsp;m × √({G.ir20mUGyH}/20). A barrier
      ninety metres out is rarely practical, which is why the usual answer is a collimator, not more
      rope. The calculation also leaves out scatter from the ground and the object being radiographed,
      so the boundary is confirmed with a survey meter during the first exposure.
    </p>

    <Faq slug="gamma-shielding" />
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
